{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld","id":1559755,"title":"Dreams in the Underworld","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-05-04T06:04:47-07:00","id":1559755,"title":"Dreams in the Underworld","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hamlet</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">To die, to sleep;<br /> To sleep: perchance to dream: ay, there's the rub;<br /> For in that sleep of death what dreams may come</div> </div> </div> </div>I've been prepping a dream journey for a crossover Chronicle for a while, and I would very much like to have one leg of it in the Underworld. While reading up, many questions came up the material (especially WTO) left unanswered. I'm pulling material from Werewolf, Mage, Wraith, and Changeling for this to fill some holes, but the main issue is that the larger picture of dreams in the Underworld are, well, underexplored.<br /> <br /> Guild Book Sandmen of course offers plenty of examples for the Phantasm Arcanos and the modus of the Guild, but there's the rub: Sandmen are largely presented as showmen, with only a few tangents on what else to do with dreams other than to provide entertaining or terifying illusions. Some of it is surely a legacy of the setting being the first, and I can't expect crossover aspects like umbral mechanics to be fully explored. There are however some fundamental things I feel could have been addressed, or I may be unaware of. Most of them concern the dreams of Wraiths themselves.<br /> <br /> First of, some basics: Wraiths do dream. Phantasm allows to watch and manipulate the dreams of the Quick as well as a Wraith in Slumber. The Underworld before the Sundering is repeatedly described as a realm of &quot;memory and dreams&quot;, as the two concepts tend to overlap. Beneath the Labyrinth, the Neverborn slumber and dream of destruction. The Sandmen know of the Oneira, mysterious dream-lords at the far end of the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> The first question goes back to the Shakespeare quote: How do the dreams of the dead differ from those of the living? Do Psyche and Shadow share the same dream, or separate ones? If so, do they overlap and distort each other? It's probable that most dreams will center around the (Dark) Passions, or at least those will have an influence.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, do Wraiths have a Chimare - or maybe two? Does the Fetter they Slumber in provide the Chimare, perhaps influencing the dream? It seems a similar concept to some Fera who maintain Den Realms, so maybe the work similarly - more so for Phantasm users, who could consciously shape them.<br /> <br /> The dream-space of the Underworld seems to be connected to the larger Dream realm, but at the same time the Sundering seems to have separated the Underworld in some capacity. Mortal dreams can not reach the Underworld as they used to, which to me at least indicates that the Shroud extends as a barrier between the dream realms of the dead and those of mortals.<br /> <br /> If you would use full crossover mechanics, dreams provide an escape route from the Underworld that many Sandmen could use instead of the more convoluted process of becoming a Risen.<br /> The Keremet are an Adhene described in C20, but these are mortals brought into the Dreaming at the moment of their death - so, more or less re-routed. I don't think there is an example of a Wraith exploring the Dreaming directly, or vice-versa, a mortal or supernatural exploring the dream realms of the dead.<br /> <br /> So how separate are those realms? Is it akin to Skinlands and Shadowlands, or a more metaphysical barrier like crossing between umbral realms? Can Oblivion reach into the Dreaming?<br /> <br /> It seems to be further complicated that Wraiths outside of the Dark Kingdom of Iron play by slightly different rules, with the Bush of Ghosts and Karta as two examples where the Wraiths are much closer to their dream realms (although the Dreamtime seems to be a special case anyways in every system).<br /> <br /> It's possible some of the questions are answered in books I don't have, if so, please let me know. In general I think that - despite having a full Guild Book - there is still much potential to explore, especially in the overlap of memory and dreams.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-05T07:00:41-07:00","id":1559787,"title":"","content":"Unfortunately, the Dreaming is one of the areas of the WoD I have the least familiarity with. What I can give you is that I've been trying to figure out why the Mage Entropy Sphere should have special properties over the Underworld and Wraiths, because it doesn't actually make sense that it should do what it does. The best I've come up with is that there are certain natural laws, tendencies, or fates that are properties of the Underworld and Entropy can affect those. So for instance, we could say Oblivion is the natural fate of all things in the Underworld and as a fate Entropy has a certain degree of influence over how quickly it approaches.<br /> <br /> If Oblivion has the character of a geas or fate, then it would have aspects that would reach into the Dreaming, though likely very badly. It does destroy dreams and imagination either by erosion or because of its natural dramatic weight, so I would imagine when it does reach into the Dreaming the results are not good.<br /> <br /> I'll also add that in Orpheus, Phantasms can actually travel by entering one person's dream and leaving another's, so there's some evidence that Wraiths can temporarily step through the Dreaming. Staying there might be a trickier proposition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-05T14:32:10-07:00","id":1559793,"title":"","content":"I always assumed that the resonance the Entropy Sphere has with Wraiths is because the Underworld and its inhabitants are entropic in nature - the Underworld is the literal last stop before either Transcendence or Oblivion, both which remove the essence of a being from this reality. But I can see the case for Oblivion being a Fate too; it is as much endpoint as it gets.<br /> The difference I see with dreams in contrast to physical energy is that dream stuff is not finite, and so entropy can not really apply. Of course, without dreamers who are themselves bound to entropy, dreams are still dependent on it as an emergent property. In a way, Oblivion might be a more 'natural' endpoint for dreams than for the dreamers.<br /> <br /> At the moment I'm leaning towards having the dream realm of the Underworld overlapping with but separated by the Shroud from the larger dream realm. It fits the narrative with the Sundering, and travel through the 'mortal' part could be restricted by the Fetters pulling the Wraith back. There are some other consequences that could be interesting (Dream Haunts, Dream Maelstroms, Tempest connection) to explore, but I'm holding back on that for now.<br /> <br /> I haven't considered Orpheus as a source, good call. I may also need to take another look at Mummy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Unfortunately, the Dreaming is one of the areas of the WoD I have the least familiarity with. </div> </div> </div>You know Wraith though, and I also welcome speculation on the other questions I had since I doubt there is a definite canon answer to them anyways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-05T17:35:32-07:00","id":1559796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm pulling material from Werewolf, Mage, Wraith, and Changeling for this to fill some holes, but the main issue is that the larger picture of dreams in the Underworld are, well, underexplored.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a very underdeveloped part of the setting and mechanics. Probably because most STs and Players aren't that interested. So whatever you decide will work as long as you like it. The only thing bad is that it can hard to figure some things out. This is how I would initially proceed, but I'd be open to changes after playtests and player feedback.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The first question goes back to the Shakespeare quote: How do the dreams of the dead differ from those of the living? Do Psyche and Shadow share the same dream, or separate ones? If so, do they overlap and distort each other? It's probable that most dreams will center around the (Dark) Passions, or at least those will have an influence.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say that since Psyche and Shadow are still the same person, they have the same dream. Dreams after all are where the conscious and subconscious mind integrate the most. The corebook is one of those that mention Dream Interpretation as one of the Dramatic Systems. Not having access to something the Shadow is telling the Psyche would eliminate some usefulness of that. Also, the Harrowing is basically a nightmare to which the Shadow subjects the Psyche.<br /> <br /> I think there should be some influence of the Shadow's Archetype on their dreams, but not necessarily a dominant one. The more permanent Angst one has, the more it'll be affected by the Shadow. Likewise the Eidolon background should also have an impact. Like everything in the Underworld, there should be some measure of the taint of Oblivion. I would also say there is greater connection to the collective unconscious and therefore the hive-mind of the spectres. An ST can have lots of fun bringing in various aspects. Personally I'd have dreams be stronger, but not necessarily more memorable except for those the ST wants to use for plot/story purposes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mechanically, do Wraiths have a Chimare - or maybe two? Does the Fetter they Slumber in provide the Chimare, perhaps influencing the dream? It seems a similar concept to some Fera who maintain Den Realms, so maybe the work similarly - more so for Phantasm users, who could consciously shape them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think do whatever works best for you. Technically, all dreams are supposed to originate in the Dream Zone, and a Chimare is good way to emulate that. New Enfants wear a plasmic Caul until it is removed, and during this time their vision is obscured and they are in a dreamlike trance. That seems connected to being in a Chimare to me. The real question is whether you want to allow other creatures to enter or leave the dreams of the dead in a crossover game. It seems to me if you want to explore this element in your games, the answer should be yes.<br /> <br /> Should the Fetter influence the dream? I don't think so. At least not normally. Although the fact that any Fetter is a psychological insight into a wraith, and that may be something the ST or Player wants to explore. In which case, do it. <br /> <br /> Also the requirements that wraiths slumber in their fetters opens a question as to what do Domems do? Domems don't have Fetters. So does that mean they do not Slumber and therefore do not dream? Do they slumber, but have their corpus remain at a deeper level of the underworld? Do they require relics or artifacts to make up for that fact? I don't think that ever gets addressed, so you'll need to decide.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The dream-space of the Underworld seems to be connected to the larger Dream realm, but at the same time the Sundering seems to have separated the Underworld in some capacity. Mortal dreams can not reach the Underworld as they used to, which to me at least indicates that the Shroud extends as a barrier between the dream realms of the dead and those of mortals.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you want it to be more difficult, just add Difficulty to any rolls.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you would use full crossover mechanics, dreams provide an escape route from the Underworld that many Sandmen could use instead of the more convoluted process of becoming a Risen.<br /> The Keremet are an Adhene described in C20, but these are mortals brought into the Dreaming at the moment of their death - so, more or less re-routed. I don't think there is an example of a Wraith exploring the Dreaming directly, or vice-versa, a mortal or supernatural exploring the dream realms of the dead.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't allow any wraith to permanently escape the Underworld. Oblivion should always be pulling them. Just like they can manifest in the Skinlands temporarily, it doesn't mean they get to stay there. Wraith's corpus is made of Plasm. That's what the Underworld is made. Not Ephemera or other types of &quot;matter&quot; that comprise the other spirit worlds.<br /> <br /> I imagine Keremets in the Dreaming are simply wraiths somehow &quot;imprisoned&quot; by fairy magic, but their stay is by definition temporary. If the fairy magic ever fails, they fall to the Underworld, perhaps becoming new enfants with no memory of their stay in the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> Personally, I like the idea that skilled practioners of Phantasm (or even Argos) might be able to temporarily enter another plane of existence. It can make for a great scene in both those other game lines and Wraith. I'd probably allow a wraith to stay in certain transitional realms for a period of time depending on their Permanent Willpower. Just long enough to have some exploration or some interesting scene. But after that, they get snapped back. Perhaps allow them to spend temporary Willpower to stay a bit longer, but always at increasing penalties.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So how separate are those realms? Is it akin to Skinlands and Shadowlands, or a more metaphysical barrier like crossing between umbral realms? Can Oblivion reach into the Dreaming?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oblivion manifests everywhere because every place knows some kind of entropy. The lower regions of the Astral map to literal hells and eventually the Labyrinth and Oblivion itself. The Wyrm and its taint appear almost everywhere in the Umbra, and I would argue that the Abyss itself eventually links directly to Oblivion.<br /> <br /> Of course, those places are all at various &quot;distances&quot; from the Oblivion and the Underworld. Certain places are obviously &quot;closer&quot; to the Underworld in some way. I'd have any travelling wraiths first arrive in those kind of places upon &quot;leaving&quot; the Underworld. I'd probably use similar description and mechanics as when Garou try to cross the Membrane. You may want to have some roll to see if the wraith can cross it. Once &quot;gone&quot;, they'd be subjected to various penalties and other effects (some of which might be a danger to others as they are bringing Oblivion with them in a way that it is not supposed to do so). The farther they try to get away from the Underworld, the worst those penalties are. I'd use as inspiration how Werewolf applies different effects to the normal rules as Garou are in the different Near Realms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld#post1559755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems to be further complicated that Wraiths outside of the Dark Kingdom of Iron play by slightly different rules, with the Bush of Ghosts and Karta as two examples where the Wraiths are much closer to their dream realms (although the Dreamtime seems to be a special case anyways in every system).</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally I would say that is less about the nature of those Dark Kingdoms, and more about the beliefs of the people whose souls go there. Technically speaking, the Skinlands are the Skinlands, and the Shadowlands are the Shadowlands. The Dark Kingdoms are simply islands in the Tempest whose rulers lay claim to the souls of people in different parts of the Skinlands/Shadowlands.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-06T06:37:39-07:00","id":1559806,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would say that since Psyche and Shadow are still the same person, they have the same dream. Dreams after all are where the conscious and subconscious mind integrate the most. The corebook is one of those that mention Dream Interpretation as one of the Dramatic Systems. Not having access to something the Shadow is telling the Psyche would eliminate some usefulness of that. Also, the Harrowing is basically a nightmare to which the Shadow subjects the Psyche.<br /> <br /> I think there should be some influence of the Shadow's Archetype on their dreams, but not necessarily a dominant one. The more permanent Angst one has, the more it'll be affected by the Shadow. Likewise the Eidolon background should also have an impact. Like everything in the Underworld, there should be some measure of the taint of Oblivion. I would also say there is greater connection to the collective unconscious and therefore the hive-mind of the spectres.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I may go with the 'one dream, two sources' option. While Shadow and Psyche belong to the same person, they are separate consciousnesses. This would also preserve the logic behind Dream Interpretation and Harrowings when only one of the two is lucid.<br /> Narrating a dream that is fueled by two minds is going to be a challenge, but it would allow me to convey some deeper concepts of Wraith nature to the players with an exposition that isn't just an info dump. It would also help to create a strangeness that underlines a categorical difference to mortal dreams.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I'd have dreams be stronger, but not necessarily more memorable except for those the ST wants to use for plot/story purposes.</div> </div> </div> </div>That seems right to me. Mortal dreams already tend to fade quickly from memory, and the proximity to Oblivion would enhance this. Where do forgotten dreams go? I would say Oblivion is a very good candidate, even if that seems a bit unfair to the Denizens of the Dreaming. But as I understand it, even the sidhe themselves don't know what happens to them after they die.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would also say there is greater connection to the collective unconscious and therefore the hive-mind of the spectres.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a pretty terrifying prospect, but would further go to explain oracular dreams of impending doom. I imagine this would be a more subtle influence than either Shadow or Psyche have on an individual's dream, but there may be Oracles or Sandmen who are more sensitive to picking up on it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think do whatever works best for you. Technically, all dreams are supposed to originate in the Dream Zone, and a Chimare is good way to emulate that. [...] The real question is whether you want to allow other creatures to enter or leave the dreams of the dead in a crossover game. It seems to me if you want to explore this element in your games, the answer should be yes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. The group doesn't have a Wraith PC, so the experience will very much be from outside-in. Introducing the concept of a Chimare also does not affect existing mechanics in WtO, so I think this is a safe addition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">New Enfants wear a plasmic Caul until it is removed, and during this time their vision is obscured and they are in a dreamlike trance. That seems connected to being in a Chimare to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>This gave me an idea for a story beat. I think I will have the group exploring the dream of a mortal while the dreamer dies of unnatural causes. This way I can transport them to Underworld dreamspace, and it is a very unique experience with good narrative potential. Witnessing the split of Psyche and Shadow could also ease the players into the concept, although I mostly want to make them panic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also the requirements that wraiths slumber in their fetters opens a question as to what do Domems do? Domems don't have Fetters. So does that mean they do not Slumber and therefore do not dream? Do they slumber, but have their corpus remain at a deeper level of the underworld? Do they require relics or artifacts to make up for that fact? I don't think that ever gets addressed, so you'll need to decide.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good question. I feel without an option to Slumber and regenerate, Domems would deteriorate too quickly. Relics or Artifacts would probably the premium option, and a Lifeweb function to create a temporary Fetter may be more common (the Level 3 Art Splice Strand seems appropriate).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bIf you want it to be more difficult, just add Difficulty to any rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I prefer a Shrouded dreamspace more for the sake of setting consistency than the added difficulty. Once dreamers have crossed, the additional roll goes away so I don't think an initial barrier would hurt the experience much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559796#post1559796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bI wouldn't allow any wraith to permanently escape the Underworld. Oblivion should always be pulling them. Just like they can manifest in the Skinlands temporarily, it doesn't mean they get to stay there. Wraith's corpus is made of Plasm. That's what the Underworld is made. Not Ephemera or other types of &quot;matter&quot; that comprise the other spirit worlds.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I agree. The Underworld is a prison realm, and escape should be near impossible - becoming Risen is a good indicator on just how difficult.<br /> Interesting side note for Spirits: from an Oneiromancer's perspective, interpreting Spirits and the Middle Umbral realms as dreams created by mundane reality itself is a pretty valid paradigm. The world dreaming other worlds, so to say.<br /> <br /> Overall, I think this has already sorted out some of the more basic questions. And while I came to this on a crossover tangent from the outside, I think it is still an interesting space to explore from the Wraith perspective itself. Rolling back the perspective to pre-Sundering times, the Far Shores and much of the Underworld landscape may have originated as dreams when mortals began to imagine the afterlife.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-06T08:27:19-07:00","id":1559807,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559793#post1559793\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always assumed that the resonance the Entropy Sphere has with Wraiths is because the Underworld and its inhabitants are entropic in nature - the Underworld is the literal last stop before either Transcendence or Oblivion, both which remove the essence of a being from this reality. But I can see the case for Oblivion being a Fate too; it is as much endpoint as it gets.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, that's close to what I'm thinking, and many mages wouldn't use the term fate, but would still consider Oblivion the natural destination of all things in the Underworld. Though in truth, Transcendence is also a destination, and it isn't supposed to be an unnatural one. Indeed it used to be the normal one.<br /> <br /> No the problem I have with Mage's take is that it acts like Entropy is not just tied to Oblivion as an end but to Oblivion as a substance. Or at least Angst as a substance. Using Entropy around wraiths tends to feed their Shadows and cause Catharsis, which is fine as a quick rule in a mage centric game but doesn't work well when we dig into the metaphysics.<br /> <br /> I have similar problems when Mage ties Entropy in to the Wyrm.<br /> <hr /><br /> Wraith Dreams<br /> <br /> Yes, the Psyche and Shadow likely share dreams. They may view them through different lenses, though, much like how they view most things through different lenses. The Eidolon likely shares the dream too, though as with most things, it doesn't play an active role. I don't think any of the three of them twist the dream in any kind of intentional sense during the actual dream, unless the dream is lucid or some specialized Phantasm power is in play. Instead I'd say that any twisting is done in hindsight, in much the same way that the Psyche and Shadow disagree about memories.<br /> <br /> Passions, dark or otherwise, likely show up frequently in dreams. I imagine that when a wraith is slumbering in a fetter, memories and feelings associated with that fetter are more likely to show up in the dream as well. Dreams are one place, though, where a wraith's monomaniacal tendencies are likely muted.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Connection Between the Dreaming and the Underworld<br /> <br /> The big question here is, what is your purpose in making travel between the Underworld and other realms difficult? That is, is it storyline based? Systems based? Metaphysics based? The reason should determine the method.<br /> <br /> By default, yes, Wraiths are largely trapped. I'd say the idea of a Shroud that separates Underworld dreams from the rest of the Dreaming is quite workable. Given ghostly connections to dreams, though, I'm inclined to say limited Dreaming access is better. Two other options here are:<br /> <br /> 1) A sort of quasi zone that is within both the Underworld and the Dreaming, which is broadly the same as the idea you suggested, but the Shroud manifests in different places for different beings. Wraiths find the Shroud between this zone and the rest of the Dreaming, changelings find the Shroud between this zone and the rest of the Underworld.<br /> <br /> 2) That wraiths can enter the Dreaming, even travel from dream to dream, but they can't exit it to anywhere but the Underworld. Or in Orpheus' case, possibly to the immaterial part of the Physical world.<br /> \u200b<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-08T05:46:07-07:00","id":1559837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559807#post1559807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Indeed, that's close to what I'm thinking, and many mages wouldn't use the term fate, but would still consider Oblivion the natural destination of all things in the Underworld. Though in truth, Transcendence is also a destination, and it isn't supposed to be an unnatural one. Indeed it used to be the normal one.<br /> <br /> No the problem I have with Mage's take is that it acts like Entropy is not just tied to Oblivion as an end but to Oblivion as a substance. Or at least Angst as a substance. Using Entropy around wraiths tends to feed their Shadows and cause Catharsis, which is fine as a quick rule in a mage centric game but doesn't work well when we dig into the metaphysics.<br /> <br /> I have similar problems when Mage ties Entropy in to the Wyrm.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bBest I can offer on this is more physics than metaphysics, but I could see the resonance of an entropic spell act as a kind of activation energy that lowers the permanent energy state of something (ie pushes it towards Entropy). Oblivion is strictly sink, never source, so I see something like Angst more like negative potential than 'substance' - it doesn't help that you have writers who treat Oblivion like an agent sometimes, or something like &quot;Powdered Angst&quot; is a thing.<br /> <br /> Having the Wyrm as an agent actually helps with that a bit. In my head I have their relationship work like this: If Oblivion is a black hole, the Wyrm is the accretion disk around it where violent, energetic stuff happens.<br /> <br /> It's possible Mage just uses language with 'inverted sign' to make the concept easier to understand, negative space can be very counter intuitive. Something like Powdered Angst could be resolved with Underworld metaphysics, perhaps like solidifying the negative potential of Angst into anti-matter to Pathos matter.<br /> <hr /><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1559755-dreams-in-the-underworld?p=1559807#post1559807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The big question here is, what is your purpose in making travel between the Underworld and other realms difficult? That is, is it storyline based? Systems based? Metaphysics based? The reason should determine the method.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right now, metaphysics. I prefer the sandbox approach and have stories be an emergent result of player interaction with the world. I can file off narrative edges ad hoc if they occur.<br /> <br /> Here's a few more points I'm pondering from having dreams as a Place within the Underworld:<br /> <br /> <b>Dream Relics</b><br /> Dreams can be a place of Memory, which is the basic building material of the Underworld. Is it conceivable that such memories could be hardened enough for Sandmen to take them into the Underworld or Shadowlands?<br /> <br /> <b>Dream Fetters</b><br /> Mortals can invest a surprising amount of passion into dreams (though usually more of the waking kind). They want to be an astronaut, but can not make it. They want to build their dream house, but never get around to it. These are the kind of unresolved passions that make Fetters. I like the idea that dreams themselves could act as Fetters which anchor a Wraith in the Shadowlands, but not from the Skinlands. On the other hand, dreams may be 'too soft' to qualify, or resolving such Fetters may be impossible.<br /> <br /> <b>Maelstroms and Tempest</b><br /> If we assume (as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> mentioned) there is a stronger connection to the collective unconscious and the Specter Hivemind, I think a Maelstrom would be able to extend into the dreams of Wraiths. Establishing a connection to the Tempest that way, the latter might sit on the 'borders' or 'between' of Chimares of individual Wraiths.<br /> <br /> Meaning, travel between individual dreams might be possible through the Tempest (and perhaps Argos or Nihils) instead of a larger dreamspace. That could be done if exiting a Chimare works like stepping sideways, with a direction of 'up' or 'down'. I think I prefer a connection rather than creating a 'Dream Tempest', as the realm is already a turmoil of fading dreams and memories. Does that make sense?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}